  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/aplocheilus-panchax-2/",
      "html": "<p>Because of the still bad flight connections, imports from India come in only sporadically. Now once again an import worked out. It contained among others the common panchax, Aplocheilus panchax. A. panchax is the most common fish in large parts of India and can be found literally everywhere, from the smallest puddle to the shores of large rivers and lakes; the panchax can even tolerate the salty water of estuaries.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/aplocheilus-panchax-2/",
      "html": "<p>The water temperatures, especially in the small waters, vary enormously: in the sun they easily rise to well over 30°C, and at night they can cool down to 12-14°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/aplocheilus-panchax-2/",
      "html": "<p>Care of the panchax is simple. They are surface-dwelling fish. The water values and the temperature play practically no role (see above).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/aplocheilus-panchax-2/",
      "html": "<p>They will happily accept any bite-sized fish food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/aplocheilus-panchax-2/",
      "html": "<p>Mostly A. panchax are peaceful, but there are sometimes quarrelsome males, so tank mates should not be too delicate. Well suited are e.g. rosy barbs and zebra danios, which come from the same biotopes. Panchax leave plants alone.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/aplocheilus-panchax-2/",
      "html": "<p>Males differ from females by the shape of the anal fin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/aplocheilus-panchax-2/",
      "html": "<p>After World War II, the panchax gradually disappeared from aquariums. People had electric heaters (instead of spirit burners under the aquarium), filters and aeration, and especially lighting. Previously, aquariums were placed at an angle to the window, so that the sunlight coming from the side illuminated the aquarium. In such light panchaxes are quite colorful, because many scales shine blue. This is quite different in fluorescent light from above, where the panchax appear quite gray, especially if they are not yet acclimated.</p>"
    }
  ],
